prs.body:
Static private accesses can fail when a method parameter or local shadows the class name, because the emitted brand check resolves to that binding. Use a per-evaluation lexical alias so classes retained from different loop iterations keep their own private brands.

For example, targeting ES2020:

```js
const classes = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
  class C {
    static #m() { return 42; }
    static has(C = this) { return #m in C; }
    static read(C = this) { return C.#m(); }
  }
  classes.push(C);
}

for (const C of classes) console.log(C.has(), C.read());
// true 42
// true 42
console.log(classes[0].has(classes[1])); // false
```

Declare the alias before computed keys and initialize it before user code in the first retained static block, or immediately after the class when static blocks are lowered. This allows checks during class evaluation without a temporal dead zone. Reparent functions extracted from computed keys to the enclosing scope.

Babel renames shadowing bindings instead, so generated output differs while the tested runtime behavior matches native JavaScript.

Fixes #27376. Supersedes #27529, building on @anzal1's original work and static-shadow fixture.

AI assistance: implementation, investigation, and validation were assisted by Codex.
